const e=[{id:1,icon:"FireExtinguisher",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Fire extinguishers",subtitle:"The PASS technique and types of extinguishers",summary:"The fire extinguisher is a key piece of equipment in civil defense. This lesson covers: types of extinguishers (water, foam, CO₂, dry powder, wet chemical and clean agent), fire classes according to the NFPA system (A, B, C, D, K), the PASS technique, monthly inspection and annual servicing, safe use and real-life scenarios. This information is for middle and high school students, so that they understand how trained adults use extinguishers. In a real fire, a student warns others, gets out and calls 115.",blocks:[{heading:"Types of extinguishers and their uses",body:`• Water extinguisher: for Class A fires (ordinary materials such as wood, paper and fabric). Water removes heat. Never use it on electrical or oil fires!
• Foam extinguisher (AFFF): for Class A and B (flammable liquids). Foam forms a layer over the fire and cuts off the oxygen. AFFF contains PFAS chemicals and is being restricted in many countries; fluorine-free foam (F3) is replacing it.
• CO₂ extinguisher: for Class B and C (liquids and electrical equipment). CO₂ cuts the fire off from oxygen and leaves no residue; it is best for electronic equipment and laboratories. CO₂ has a short range and is not for deep-seated wood or paper fires. Hold only the handle, never the horn, which gets very cold. In a small room, leave and ventilate after use, because CO₂ pushes out the oxygen.
• Dry powder extinguisher (dry chemical, ABC): for Class A, B and C. It is the most common type. The chemical powder stops the chemical reaction of the fire. Note: ABC powder is not suitable for Class D (metals) or Class K (cooking oil).
• Wet chemical extinguisher: specifically for Class K (kitchen fires and frying oil). It turns the oil into a soap-like substance (saponification) and forms a protective layer over it.
• Clean agent extinguisher: for sensitive equipment such as computers and servers. It leaves no residue and does not damage the equipment.`,image:"media/images/ext_types_all.png"},{heading:"The PASS technique (step by step)",body:`This is how trained adults use an extinguisher. Students do not fight fires: they warn others, get out and call 115.

Before you use an extinguisher (every answer must be yes):
• The fire is small and just starting, like a fire in a wastebasket or a pan.
• The extinguisher is the right type for this fire.
• Your exit is behind you and the fire is not between you and the exit.
• The alarm has been raised: others are warned and 115 has been called.
• You are trained to use the extinguisher.
• No smoke or heat is blocking your way out.
• If the fire does not go out at once, stop, get out and close the door behind you.

P = Pull: pull out the safety pin. This pin locks the extinguisher handle.

A = Aim: aim the nozzle at the base of the fire, not at the top of the flames! If you aim at the top of the fire, it will not go out and it may spread.

S = Squeeze: squeeze the handle slowly and firmly. If you let go of the handle, the agent stops; this way you stay in control.

S = Sweep: sweep the nozzle from side to side at the base of the fire until the fire is completely out. If the fire flares up again, repeat PASS.

Safe distance: start from the distance shown on the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.

PASS is the general method for most hand-held extinguishers. Class D, wet chemical, clean agent and wheeled units: follow the label and the manufacturer's instructions.`,image:"media/images/pass_detailed.png"},{heading:"Monthly inspection and annual servicing",body:`Monthly inspection (visual):
• Location: the extinguisher is in its place, clearly visible, mounted on the wall and near a door; its top is no higher than 1.5 meters above the floor (NFPA 10)
• Pressure gauge, if there is one: the needle is in the green zone. CO₂ extinguishers have no gauge: lift one to feel that it is full
• Safety pin and plastic seal: in place and unbroken
• Label: readable and facing out
• Nozzle and hose: no breaks, no cracks and no blockages
• Extinguisher body: no rust, no dents, no leaks and no damage
• Service tag: shows the date of the last annual service

Annual servicing and recharging:
• Inspection and maintenance: a qualified technician inspects and services the extinguishers every year
• Recharge: after every use (even a short one), a qualified technician must recharge the extinguisher immediately
• 6-year maintenance: stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers that need a 12-year hydrostatic test are emptied and checked inside every 6 years
• Hydrostatic test (pressure test of the cylinder), by type (NFPA 10): every 5 years for water, foam (AFFF), CO₂ and stainless-steel dry chemical extinguishers; every 12 years for stored-pressure dry chemical extinguishers with mild steel, brazed brass or aluminum shells. This is a test, not a replacement of the extinguisher. The rules for Duhok are not yet confirmed`,image:"media/images/ext_inspection.png"}],examples:["The ABC extinguisher is the most common type, for homes and schools.","CO₂ for laboratories and electronic equipment.","Wet chemical is specifically for kitchen fires (K).","Distance in the PASS technique: start from the distance on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you.","Monthly inspection: pin + seal + label + hose + service tag + gauge (if there is one).","Recharge after every use; professional servicing every year.","Always keep your exit route behind you.","For a home, adults choose an extinguisher they can lift and use; the weight is on the label."],tips:["Color is not the fire class. Under the European standard BS EN 3 the extinguisher body is red; in the UK a colored band (BS 7863) shows the agent: cream = foam, blue = dry powder, black = CO₂, yellow = wet chemical; water has no band. Other countries use other colors and the color practice in Duhok is not yet confirmed, so always read the label and its class symbols.",'First the "Before you use an extinguisher" check (every answer must be yes), then PASS: Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep.',"Distance: start from the distance on the extinguisher label, with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller.","Always keep your exit route behind you.","If the fire does not get smaller in the first few seconds, or the extinguisher runs empty, get out immediately and call 115."],warnings:["Never use a water extinguisher on electrical fires (C) or hot oil fires (K)! Water conducts electricity and spreads hot oil violently. Use an extinguisher only on small fires in their early stage; do not use it if the fire is bigger than a wastebasket, is spreading or smoke is building up: get out immediately and call 115!"],realCase:"In a kitchen in Duhok, frying oil caught fire. A trained staff member used a wet chemical (K) extinguisher, following the instructions on its label, and put out the fire. If a water extinguisher had been used, the fire would have spread violently and caused a major disaster. Knowing the types of extinguishers saves lives!",video:"media/videos/lesson_extinguisher_full.mp4",game:{instruction:"Which fire class is each type of extinguisher for? Choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"CO₂ extinguisher → electrical fire (C)",emoji:"🔵",correct:!0},{text:"Water extinguisher → kitchen oil fire (K)",emoji:"💧",correct:!1},{text:"ABC extinguisher → Class A, B and C fires",emoji:"🔴",correct:!0},{text:"Foam extinguisher → metal fire (D)",emoji:"🟡",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"You are in the chemistry laboratory at school. Suddenly a fire starts on a bench: a flammable organic liquid (Class B) has caught fire, and it is close to electrical equipment (Class C). There are two extinguishers there: a water extinguisher and a CO₂ extinguisher. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_lab_fire.png",options:[{text:'I shout "Fire!" to warn the teacher and my classmates, we leave the lab by the nearest exit and close the door behind us, and I call 115 from outside. Only the teacher or trained staff decide whether to use the CO₂ extinguisher on a small fire.',correct:!0,feedback:"This is the student's job: warn others, get out, close the door and call 115; trained staff decide about the extinguisher."},{text:"I use the water extinguisher on the fire",correct:!1,feedback:"Water is wrong for burning liquids and conducts electricity, and students do not fight fires: warn others, get out and call 115."},{text:"I use the CO₂ extinguisher on the fire myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Students do not fight fires. Warn others, get out and call 115."},{text:"I leave on my own without alerting the teacher or my classmates",correct:!1,feedback:'Getting out is right, but others must be warned too: shout "Fire!", help everyone leave and call 115 from outside.'}]},quiz:[{question:"What does PASS stand for?",options:[{text:"Pull, Aim, Squeeze, Sweep",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Pull the pin, aim at the base, squeeze the handle, sweep from side to side."},{text:"Push, Aim, Spray, Stop",correct:!1,feedback:"PASS starts with Pull (the safety pin) and ends with Sweep; Push, Spray and Stop are not PASS steps."},{text:"Pull, Attack, Spray, Sweep",correct:!1,feedback:"A means Aim at the base of the fire, and the first S means Squeeze the handle, not Attack or Spray."}],explanation:"PASS means Pull the pin, Aim at the base, Squeeze the handle, Sweep side to side. Trained adults use it only when every pre-use answer is yes; students warn others, get out and call 115."},{question:"Which extinguisher is best for electrical fires (C)?",options:[{text:"CO₂",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! CO₂ leaves no residue and does not conduct electricity."},{text:"Water",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Water conducts electricity."},{text:"Wet chemical (K)",correct:!1,feedback:"No! That is for kitchen fires."}]},{question:"What is a wet chemical extinguisher specifically for?",options:[{text:"Frying oil and kitchen fires",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! It turns the oil into a soap-like substance and smothers the fire."},{text:"Ordinary fires such as wood fires",correct:!1,feedback:"Wood and paper fires are Class A, for water, foam or ABC powder; wet chemical is made for cooking oil (K)."},{text:"Metal fires (D)",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Class D needs an agent whose label lists that metal."}],explanation:"Wet chemical is made for Class K: it turns burning cooking oil into a soap-like substance and forms a layer over it. Ordinary (A) and metal (D) fires need other agents."},{question:"In the PASS technique, where do you find how far from the fire to stand?",options:[{text:"On the extinguisher label or in the manufacturer's instructions",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Start from that distance with your exit behind you, and move closer only as the fire gets smaller."},{text:"One fixed distance is used for every extinguisher, whatever its size",correct:!1,feedback:"No. The distance depends on the type and size of the extinguisher; it is on the label."},{text:"As close as possible, so that the powder reaches the flames",correct:!1,feedback:"No, that is dangerous!"}]},{question:"When should extinguishers be inspected?",options:[{text:"Every month by eye and every year by a qualified technician",correct:!0,feedback:"A monthly visual check finds problems early, and a qualified technician inspects and services each extinguisher every year."},{text:"Only after they have been used, so that they can be refilled",correct:!1,feedback:"An extinguisher must be ready before a fire, so it is checked every month; after any use it is recharged at once."},{text:"Once every 5 years, when a qualified technician tests them",correct:!1,feedback:"No! That is the hydrostatic test interval for some types, not the routine inspection."}],explanation:"Extinguishers get a visual check every month and an inspection and service by a qualified technician every year. The hydrostatic test every 5 or 12 years, by type, is a separate test."}]},{id:2,icon:"FlameKindling",accent:"#ea580c",title:"Fire Classes A-B-C-D-K",subtitle:"Recognizing types of fire and extinguishing methods",summary:"Not all fires are the same. Each type of fire needs a different method. Knowing the fire classes is the foundation of every firefighter's work. This lesson uses the letters of the American NFPA 10 system (A, B, C, D, K) as the project reference; the official system for Duhok is not yet confirmed. In the European EN 2 system the letters are different: NFPA A = EN A; NFPA B (liquids) = EN B; NFPA B (gases) = EN C; NFPA C (live electrical equipment) has no EN class; NFPA D = EN D; NFPA K = EN F. If you use the wrong method, the fire will get worse.",blocks:[{heading:"Class A: ordinary materials",body:`• What it is: wood, paper, fabric, plastic; ordinary solid materials that burn
• Method: water (cooling), foam, ABC powder
• Symbol (NFPA): a green triangle with the letter A
• Examples: house fires, trash bins, curtains`,image:"media/images/class_a.png"},{heading:"Classes B, C, D and K (NFPA)",body:`• B: flammable liquids and gases: gasoline, oil, alcohol, thinner, gas. Liquids: foam, CO₂ or ABC powder; for alcohol and other polar solvents: CO₂, ABC powder or alcohol-resistant (AR) foam, because ordinary foam breaks down on them. Gas: stop the gas first if it is safe; never put out a gas flame you cannot stop. Never use water!
• C: live electrical equipment: wiring, appliances and electrical panels. First, if you can do so safely, cut off the electricity. Method: CO₂, ABC powder.
• D: combustible metals: magnesium, titanium, sodium. Method: only an extinguisher or agent whose label lists that metal; the agent differs from metal to metal. Never use water, ordinary powder or foam!
• K: hot cooking oil. Method: wet chemical, or put a lid on the pan and turn off the heat under the pan. Water is a disaster and ABC powder is not enough!`,image:"media/images/class_bcdk.png"},{heading:"The fire triangle and extinguishing methods",body:`Fire is made up of three things: heat + fuel + oxygen (O₂). If one of them is removed, the fire goes out. A fourth element, the chemical chain reaction, makes the fire tetrahedron; ABC powder works mainly by stopping it.
• Cooling: water lowers the heat of the fire → Class A
• Smothering: foam or CO₂ cut the fire off from oxygen → Class B
• Removing the fuel: move the fuel away from the fire, for example by closing the gas valve
• Stopping the chain reaction: chemical powder stops the chemical reaction of the fire → ABC powder`,image:"media/images/fire_triangle_adv.png"}],examples:["A = wood and paper → water.","B = gasoline → foam or CO₂; gas → stop the gas first, if it is safe.","C = electricity → CO₂.","D = metals → only an agent whose label lists that metal.","K = hot oil → wet chemical.","Water + hot oil = explosion and spreading fire!","Water + electricity = electric shock!","Fire triangle: heat + fuel + O₂."],tips:["Learn to recognize the fire classes by their symbols.","Water is only for Class A.","CO₂ is for Class B and C.","Wet chemical is for Class K."],warnings:["Never pour water on an oil fire (K)! Under hot oil, water instantly turns to steam, throws the burning oil violently upward and a large fire spreads quickly. This is extremely dangerous."],realCase:"At a factory in Erbil, a metal fire (Class D, magnesium) broke out. A worker tried to put it out with water, but the fire grew violently larger! A specialist then used a Class D powder whose label lists magnesium and put the fire out. Knowing the fire classes saves lives!",video:"media/videos/lesson_fire_classes.mp4",game:{instruction:"Match each fire class with its correct extinguishing method:",items:[{text:"Hot oil (K) → wet chemical",emoji:"🍳",correct:!0},{text:"Paper (A) → water",emoji:"📄",correct:!0},{text:"Gasoline (B) → foam or CO₂",emoji:"⛽",correct:!0},{text:"Electricity (C) → water",emoji:"⚡",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"Frying oil has caught fire in the school kitchen. There is a water extinguisher and a wet chemical (K) extinguisher there. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_kitchen_fire.png",options:[{text:'I keep away from the stove, shout "Fire!" to warn the kitchen staff and a teacher, get out and call 115. A trained adult turns off the stove and puts a lid on the pan, or uses the wet chemical (K) extinguisher.',correct:!0,feedback:"Hot oil fires are for trained adults: keeping away, raising the alarm, getting out and calling 115 is the student's job."},{text:"I pour water onto the hot oil",correct:!1,feedback:"Water under hot oil turns to steam at once and throws burning oil upward; never pour water on an oil fire."},{text:"I go to the pan and use the wet chemical (K) extinguisher myself",correct:!1,feedback:"Students do not go near a burning pan; this is a job for trained adults. Get out and call 115."},{text:"I pick up the burning pan and carry it outside",correct:!1,feedback:"Moving a burning pan can splash burning oil and spread the fire; keep away from the stove, warn others and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"What happens if water is poured onto an oil fire (K)?",options:[{text:"An explosion and the fire spreads",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! The water turns to steam and spreads the burning oil."},{text:"The fire goes out as the oil cools",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not put out an oil fire: it turns to steam under the oil and spreads the flames violently."},{text:"The fire stays the same size",correct:!1,feedback:"Something dangerous happens: the water turns to steam at once and throws burning oil upward, spreading the fire."}],explanation:"Under hot oil, water instantly turns to steam and throws the burning oil upward, so the fire spreads fast. Never pour water on a Class K fire."},{question:"In a Class C (electrical) fire, what is the first step?",options:[{text:"Cut off the electricity, if it is safe",correct:!0,feedback:"Right: cut the power first if it is safe; then warn others, get out and call 115."},{text:"Pour water on the equipment at once",correct:!1,feedback:"Water conducts electricity, so pouring it on live equipment can cause an electric shock."},{text:"Wait for the fire to go out all by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Doing nothing lets the fire grow: warn others, get out and call 115."}],explanation:"In a Class C fire the equipment is live, so the first step is to cut off the electricity if it is safe; never use water. Students warn others, get out and call 115."},{question:"What is the fire triangle made up of?",options:[{text:"Heat + fuel + oxygen",correct:!0,feedback:"These three together make fire; removing any one of them, by cooling, smothering or removing the fuel, puts it out."},{text:"Water + wind + heat",correct:!1,feedback:"Water and wind are not sides of the triangle; the three sides are heat, fuel and oxygen."},{text:"Smoke + fire + oxygen",correct:!1,feedback:"Smoke and fire are not sides of the triangle; the three sides are heat, fuel and oxygen."}],explanation:"Fire needs heat, fuel and oxygen together; if one of them is removed, the fire goes out. This is why cooling, smothering and removing the fuel work."}]},{id:3,icon:"Activity",accent:"#d97706",title:"Earthquakes: advanced level",subtitle:"Building safety and rescue",summary:'At the advanced level, we do not only learn "Drop, Cover, Hold On"; we also learn how buildings are built, how to check for damage after an earthquake, the principles of rescuing people trapped under rubble and how to support rescue teams.',blocks:[{heading:"Construction and structural safety",body:`• Buildings constructed without earthquake engineering (Seismic Engineering) are the most dangerous
• Weak points: an open ground floor (soft story), old columns, foundations without concrete
• The Kurdistan Region lies on the Zagros mountain belt, which is a seismically active zone; therefore earthquakes can happen here
• During the shaking: Drop to the ground, take Cover under a sturdy table and Hold On (Drop, Cover, Hold On), away from windows and heavy equipment. Do not go into a doorway and do not trust the "triangle of life"
• After an earthquake, inspect buildings: cracks in walls, leaning columns, damage to foundations`,image:"media/images/seismic_building.png"},{heading:"Rescue from rubble (urban SAR)",body:`• Rescuing people from under rubble is the job of trained Civil Defense teams (115). Students never climb onto rubble or go into damaged buildings, even with gloves and a helmet: rubble can shift and collapse
• From a safe place, listen and call out, note where a voice comes from and tell the rescue team
• Keep other people away and follow the rescue team's instructions
• Be aware: gas, electricity and water can become hazards
• For information: rescue teams sort casualties by color (Triage): red = urgent, yellow = can wait, green = minor injury, black = dead or beyond help. Students do not do this; they report and help only when the team asks`,image:"media/images/urban_sar.png"},{heading:"After the shaking: utilities, damaged buildings and aftershocks",body:`• Expect aftershocks: if the ground shakes again, Drop, Cover, Hold On again
• Check yourself and the people around you for injuries; if people are trapped or injured, call 115 and 122
• If you smell gas or hear it leaking: do not strike matches or lighters and do not touch any switch; close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, without going into the area where the gas has collected; otherwise do not go near it. Get out, warn others and call 115 from outside
• If you are in a damaged building, get out and move away from it; do not go back in until specialists have checked it
• Stay away from fallen power lines and anything touching them, and call 115
• Listen to official announcements on a battery-powered radio, television or your phone, and follow the instructions of Civil Defense
• For the emergency kit, see the lesson "The emergency kit" in Community awareness`,image:"media/images/eq_after_shaking.png"}],examples:["Building inspection: cracks, columns, foundations.","Rescue: listen from a safe place and report; never climb onto rubble.","Emergency kit: water + food + medicine + flashlight.","Close the gas valve only if you smell gas or hear it leaking, and only if you can reach it safely.","Use a radio to listen for warnings.","Triage (rescue teams): red, yellow, green, black.","Be prepared for 72 hours.","Agree on an assembly point."],tips:["Always keep your emergency kit ready.","During the shaking: Drop, Cover, Hold On.","Do not use the elevator.","Stay outside until specialists have inspected the building."],warnings:["After the first earthquake, always expect aftershocks. Do not go into damaged buildings! If you smell gas or hear it leaking: do not touch any switch or flame, close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, then get out and call 115 from outside. Do not use tap water for drinking until it has been tested."],realCase:"On November 12, 2017, a strong earthquake (Mw 7.3, according to the USGS) struck near the Iran–Iraq border, south of Halabja. In an earthquake like this, trained volunteers can help the rescue teams: they know how to listen for the voices of people trapped under rubble and how to sort casualties using the Triage system. Knowledge saves lives!",video:"media/videos/lesson_earthquake_adv.mp4",game:{instruction:"After an earthquake, which actions are correct?",items:[{text:"If you smell gas, get out and call 115 from outside",emoji:"🚪",correct:!0},{text:"Go into the damaged building",emoji:"🏠",correct:!1},{text:"Tell the rescue team where you heard a voice",emoji:"📢",correct:!0},{text:"Use the elevator",emoji:"🛗",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"An earthquake of magnitude 6.2 (on the Richter scale) has struck. You are at school. A nearby building has cracked and you can hear someone's voice coming from under the rubble. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_eq_adv.png",options:[{text:"I immediately alert the teacher and we call 115 and 122. I mark where the voice is coming from and talk loudly to the injured person, but I do not go into the cracked building until the rescue teams arrive",correct:!0,feedback:"Alerting the teacher, calling 115 and 122 and marking the voice for rescuers helps most; only trained teams go into damaged buildings."},{text:"I go into the cracked building on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"Rubble can shift and collapse; students never go into damaged buildings, even with gloves and a helmet. Report the voice to rescuers."},{text:"I do nothing and just wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Someone trapped needs help fast: alert the teacher, call 115 and 122, and tell the rescue team where the voice is."},{text:"I run home",correct:!1,feedback:"Running away leaves the trapped person without help; stay in a safe place, alert the teacher and report the voice to rescuers."}]},quiz:[{question:"What does Triage mean?",options:[{text:"Sorting casualties by the priority and urgency of their condition",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Rescue teams use red = urgent, yellow = can wait, green = minor, black = dead or beyond help."},{text:"Giving first aid to each casualty in the order in which they arrive",correct:!1,feedback:"Triage is not treatment: it means sorting casualties by the priority and urgency of their condition."},{text:"Getting everyone out of the building by the nearest safe exit",correct:!1,feedback:"Triage is not about leaving a building: it means rescue teams sort casualties by urgency, using colors."}],explanation:"Triage means rescue teams sort casualties by how urgent their condition is, using colors: red, yellow, green and black. Students do not triage; they report and help only when the team asks."},{question:"After an earthquake, when do you close the gas valve?",options:[{text:"Only if you smell or hear gas and can reach the valve safely",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Then get out and call 115 from outside."},{text:"Every time, before anything else, even if there is no smell",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Only if there are signs of a gas leak."},{text:"Never, even if you smell gas and the valve is right beside you",correct:!1,feedback:"No! If you smell gas and the valve can be reached safely, close it; if not, do not go near it: get out and call 115 from outside."}]},{question:"After the shaking, you are inside a cracked, damaged building. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Get out, move away from the building and stay out until specialists have checked it",correct:!0},{text:"Stay inside near a wall until the aftershocks stop, then decide whether to leave",correct:!1},{text:"Go back in quickly to collect your bag and your documents, then wait outside for news",correct:!1}],explanation:"Damaged buildings can collapse, especially in aftershocks. Get out, move away and do not go back in until specialists have checked the building."}]},{id:4,icon:"HeartPulse",accent:"#e11d48",title:"First aid: cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)",subtitle:"Chest compressions, rescue breaths and the AED",summary:"CPR (Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation), made up of chest compressions and rescue breaths, is a life-saving technique used when the heart and breathing stop. Everyone should learn CPR. According to the American Heart Association (AHA), for every minute without CPR and an AED, the chance of survival falls by about 7–10%. The C-A-B order: first chest Compressions, then opening the Airway, then Breathing.",blocks:[{heading:"CPR for adults (30:2)",body:`1. Safety: check your surroundings
2. Responsiveness: shake the person's shoulders and ask loudly, "Are you all right?"
3. Call 122 (ambulance) and ask for an AED; put the phone on speaker
4. Check breathing: look at the chest for 10 seconds or less
5. 30 chest compressions: in the center of the chest, 5–6 cm deep, 100–120 compressions per minute; after each compression, let the chest recoil fully
6. 2 rescue breaths, if you are trained: tilt the head back and lift the chin; blow into the mouth for 1 second per breath, until the chest rises. If you are not trained, or not willing to give breaths: chest compressions only (Hands-Only CPR), without stopping
7. Continue 30:2 (or compressions only) until the ambulance arrives or an AED is brought`,image:"media/images/cpr_steps.png"},{heading:"The AED (defibrillator)",body:`• An AED is an automatic device that checks the heart rhythm and gives an electric shock if one is needed
• Switch the device on and follow its voice instructions
• AED pads: one on the upper right side of the chest (just below the collarbone), the other on the lower left side of the chest (toward the side of the body)
• If the AED says "shock advised": make sure no one is touching the casualty, then press the shock button
• After the shock, immediately resume CPR for 2 minutes
• The AED checks the heart rhythm again every 2 minutes`,image:"media/images/aed_use.png"},{heading:"CPR for children and infants",body:`• Child (from 1 year old until puberty): use one or two hands, about 5 cm deep (one third of the depth of the chest); 30:2 if you are alone, 15:2 if there are two rescuers
• Infant (under 1 year old): two thumbs on the chest with both hands encircling it (two-thumb encircling), or the heel of one hand; about 4 cm deep (one third of the depth of the chest); 30:2 alone, 15:2 with two rescuers. The two-finger method is no longer taught (AHA 2025)
• Breaths for an infant: place your mouth over the infant's mouth and nose together and blow gently
• Children, infants and people who drowned especially need breaths: if you are trained and able, give CPR with breaths; if you cannot, compressions alone are better than nothing
• If you are alone: if you have a phone with you, call 122 immediately on speaker and start CPR; if you have no phone, do 2 minutes of CPR, then go and call 122
• Choking: child: 5 back blows + 5 abdominal thrusts (above the navel, well below the breastbone); infant: 5 back blows (head lower than the chest) + 5 chest thrusts; never give abdominal thrusts to an infant`,image:"media/images/cpr_child.png"}],examples:["30 compressions + 2 breaths = 1 cycle.","Depth: 5–6 cm (adult), about 5 cm (child), about 4 cm (infant).","Rate: 100–120 compressions per minute.","First: call 122 and ask for an AED.","AED: place the pads on the chest and follow the instructions.","Every minute without CPR: the chance of survival falls by about 7–10%.","Infant: two thumbs or the heel of one hand; mouth and nose together.","If there are two people: one gives compressions and the other gets the AED ready."],tips:["Always put your own safety first.","Memorize 30:2.","Push hard and fast: 5–6 cm, 100–120 per minute.","Let the chest recoil fully between compressions.","If an AED is available, use it as soon as possible."],warnings:["Start CPR early! For every minute without CPR, the chance of survival falls by about 7–10%, and after 10 minutes without CPR the chance becomes very low. Do not be afraid of breaking ribs: ribs may break during correct CPR, but life matters more!"],realCase:"In a supermarket in Duhok, a 55-year-old man suddenly collapsed and his heart stopped. A Grade 11 student who had learned CPR immediately started chest compressions while his friend called 122. The student kept up the compressions without stopping until the ambulance arrived and the paramedics took over. Without CPR, the man might have died.",video:"media/videos/lesson_cpr.mp4",game:{instruction:"The CPR steps in the right order: choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"Safety → responsiveness → 122 → compressions",emoji:"✅",correct:!0},{text:"Compressions → breaths → 122",emoji:"❌",correct:!1},{text:"30 compressions + 2 breaths",emoji:"💓",correct:!0},{text:"10 compressions + 5 breaths",emoji:"❌",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"At school, a student suddenly collapses, does not respond and is not breathing normally. You are the first person there. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_cpr.png",options:[{text:"I check that the scene is safe, test whether he responds, call 122 and ask for an AED, check his breathing and start CPR at 30:2 until the ambulance arrives",correct:!0,feedback:"This is the right order: safe scene, response, 122 and an AED, breathing check, then CPR; if not trained, give compressions only."},{text:"I pour water on his face",correct:!1,feedback:"Water on the face does not help someone who is not breathing normally; call 122, ask for an AED and start CPR."},{text:"I let him sleep",correct:!1,feedback:"This is not sleep: no response and no normal breathing mean you call 122, ask for an AED and start CPR."},{text:"I run away",correct:!1,feedback:"Every minute without CPR lowers his chance of survival; stay, call 122, ask for an AED and push hard and fast."}]},quiz:[{question:"What is the ratio of compressions to breaths in adult CPR?",options:[{text:"30 compressions + 2 breaths",correct:!0,feedback:"Adult CPR is 30 compressions, then 2 breaths if you are trained; if not, compressions only without stopping."},{text:"15 compressions + 2 breaths",correct:!1,feedback:"No! 15:2 is for children and infants when there are two rescuers."},{text:"10 compressions + 1 breath",correct:!1,feedback:"That ratio is not taught: adult CPR is 30 compressions and 2 breaths, or compressions only (Hands-Only CPR)."}],explanation:"For adults, trained rescuers give 30 chest compressions and 2 rescue breaths (30:2). If you are not trained or not willing to give breaths, give compressions only, without stopping (Hands-Only CPR)."},{question:"What is the rate of chest compressions in CPR?",options:[{text:"100–120 per minute",correct:!0,feedback:"100–120 per minute is the right rate; push hard and fast and let the chest recoil fully each time."},{text:"60–80 per minute",correct:!1,feedback:"That is too slow!"},{text:"150–180 per minute",correct:!1,feedback:"That is too fast!"}],explanation:"CPR compressions are given at 100–120 per minute: push hard and fast, 5–6 cm deep for adults, and let the chest recoil fully after each one."},{question:"For every minute without CPR, by about how much does the chance of survival fall?",options:[{text:"7–10%",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! (AHA)"},{text:"1–2%",correct:!1,feedback:"The loss is much faster: about 7–10% for every minute without CPR and an AED (AHA)."},{text:"40–50%",correct:!1,feedback:"The AHA figure is about 7–10% per minute, not 40–50%; still, every minute counts, so start CPR early."}],explanation:"According to the AHA, the chance of survival falls by about 7–10% for every minute without CPR and an AED. That is why you call 122 and start CPR at once."}]},{id:5,icon:"Biohazard",accent:"#9333ea",title:"Chemical and hazardous materials (HazMat)",subtitle:"Identification and handling",summary:"HazMat (hazardous materials) are dangerous substances: chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear (CBRN), as well as explosive materials. Learning to recognize them and to handle them correctly is important for middle and high school students.",blocks:[{heading:"GHS and hazardous material symbols",body:`GHS (Globally Harmonized System): the worldwide system for classifying and labeling chemicals. It has 9 pictograms:
• Skull and crossbones = toxic and deadly
• Flame = flammable
• Flame over circle = oxidizer
• Exploding bomb = explosive
• Drops on a hand and on metal = corrosive (Corrosive)
• Gas cylinder = gas under pressure
• Exclamation mark (!) = irritant and minor harm
• Human body with a star on the chest = serious health hazard (such as cancer)
• Fish and tree = hazard to the environment

GHS has no pictogram for radioactive materials; that symbol is found in the UN regulations for the transport of dangerous goods (Class 7).

The NFPA 704 diamond (4 colors):
• Red = flammability (0–4)
• Blue = health (0–4)
• Yellow = instability (0–4)
• White = special hazards: OX (oxidizer), W with a line through it (do not use water), SA (simple asphyxiant gas)`,image:"media/images/ghs_symbols.png"},{heading:"SDS and personal protective equipment (PPE)",body:`SDS (Safety Data Sheet): the safety information sheet, which contains 16 sections of information about each chemical:
• Section 2: hazard identification
• Section 4: first aid
• Section 5: firefighting measures
• Section 8: personal protective equipment (PPE)

PPE levels:
• A: fully encapsulating chemical suit + self-contained breathing apparatus (SCBA)
• B: chemical-protective clothing + SCBA
• C: chemical-protective clothing + filter mask
• D: ordinary work clothes + gloves and goggles`,image:"media/images/sds_ppe.png"},{heading:"Decontamination and procedure",body:`If a hazardous material is released:
1. Raise the alarm: call 115 (and 122 as well if there are casualties)
2. Get out: move to the side the wind is coming from (upwind), so that the wind does not carry gas and dust toward you
3. Move as far away as the authorities tell you, and follow their instructions
4. Do not touch it and do not smell it
5. Decontamination (remove, remove, remove): move away into fresh air, take off your outer clothing and blot or brush the substance off the skin with a dry cloth or tissue; if the skin itches or burns, rinse it with plenty of water. If it gets into your eyes, rinse them with clean water
6. Wait for the Civil Defense HazMat team`,image:"media/images/decontamination.png"}],examples:["Skull = toxic.","Flame = flammable.","Drops on a hand = corrosive.","Exploding bomb = explosive.","SDS: 16 sections.","PPE levels: A, B, C, D.","Move to the side the wind is coming from (upwind).","Move as far away as the authorities tell you."],tips:["Learn the 9 GHS pictograms.","Read the SDS before using any substance.","Use the appropriate PPE.","Move to the side the wind is coming from."],warnings:["Never touch, smell or taste an unknown chemical! Always move to the side the wind is coming from (upwind) and stay as far away as the authorities tell you. Call 115 so that the HazMat team comes!"],realCase:"At a factory in Sulaymaniyah, a quantity of acid spilled. A trained worker immediately raised the alarm, moved all the workers to the side the wind was coming from and called 115 so that the HazMat team would come. His training had taught him exactly what to do.",video:"media/videos/lesson_hazmat.mp4",game:{instruction:"Match each symbol with its meaning; choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"Skull and crossbones = toxic",emoji:"☠️",correct:!0},{text:"Flame = a harmless substance",emoji:"🔥",correct:!1},{text:"Corrosive symbol = harmless to the skin",emoji:"⚗️",correct:!1},{text:"Exclamation mark (!) = health hazard (irritant)",emoji:"⚠️",correct:!0}]},activity:{prompt:"In the laboratory, a bottle of acid has broken and spilled. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_hazmat.png",options:[{text:"I warn everyone, we leave the laboratory toward the side the wind is coming from, I alert the teacher and we call 115, and I do not touch it",correct:!0,feedback:"Warning everyone, leaving upwind, telling the teacher, calling 115 and not touching the acid follows the lesson's procedure exactly."},{text:"I clean it up with a cloth",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not touch a spilled chemical, even with a cloth; leave upwind, warn others, call 115 and wait for the HazMat team."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not treat the spill yourself: do not touch it, get out upwind, call 115 and wait for the HazMat team."},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"Staying near a spill puts everyone at risk: warn others, leave upwind, tell the teacher and call 115."}]},quiz:[{question:"In the NFPA diamond, what does the color red mean?",options:[{text:"Flammability",correct:!0,feedback:"Red shows flammability, rated from 0 to 4; the higher the number, the more easily the material catches fire."},{text:"Health hazard",correct:!1,feedback:"That is blue!"},{text:"Instability",correct:!1,feedback:"That is yellow!"}],explanation:"In the NFPA 704 diamond, red = flammability, blue = health, yellow = instability (each rated 0–4) and white = special hazards such as OX, W or SA."},{question:"Why do we move to the side the wind is coming from (upwind)?",options:[{text:"So that the wind does not carry gas and dust toward us",correct:!0,feedback:"Upwind, the wind blows the gas and dust away from you instead of carrying them toward you."},{text:"So that we reach home or the assembly point sooner",correct:!1,feedback:"The direction is chosen for safety, not convenience: upwind keeps the gas and dust from blowing toward you."},{text:"It makes no difference which way the wind is blowing from",correct:!1,feedback:"It matters a lot: if the wind blows toward you, it carries the gas and dust to you."}],explanation:"Moving to the side the wind is coming from (upwind) means the wind carries gas and dust away from you, not toward you. Then stay as far away as the authorities tell you."},{question:"A chemical splashes onto your arm and clothes. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Move into fresh air, take off the outer clothing and blot the substance off with a dry cloth",correct:!0},{text:"Keep the clothes on so that they protect the skin, and wait without moving for the HazMat team",correct:!1},{text:"Wipe the liquid off with your bare hand and carry on, because a small amount cannot cause harm",correct:!1}],explanation:"Remove yourself, remove outer clothing, remove the substance: blot or brush it off the skin with a dry cloth. If the skin itches or burns, rinse it with plenty of water; if it gets into your eyes, rinse them with clean water."}]},{id:6,icon:"Waves",accent:"#0891b2",title:"Floods and disasters: planning",subtitle:"Warning and preparedness",summary:"Floods and natural disasters require advance planning. At the high school level, we learn about warning systems, the family emergency plan and the role of students in safety teams.",blocks:[{heading:"Warning systems",body:`• EWS (Early Warning System): a system that gives early warning before a disaster
• Follow official warnings and announcements, and do what they tell you
• Warning levels and channels differ from country to country: learn the system that the authorities in your area use
• Flood gauge: shows the height of the water
• Weather warnings: through radio, television and cell phones`,image:"media/images/ews_system.png"},{heading:"Family emergency plan",body:`• Assembly points: 2 points (one nearby and one farther away)
• Exit routes: 2 routes out of the home
• Communication: the phone numbers of all family members
• Emergency kit: for 72 hours (water: about 4 liters per person per day)
• Documents: copies kept in a safe place away from water
• Practice: test the plan with your family at least twice a year`,image:"media/images/family_plan.png"},{heading:"The student's role in safety",body:`• CERT (Community Emergency Response Team): community volunteer teams that help during emergencies
• Trained students can become first helpers
• Roles: raising the alarm, first aid, helping affected people
• Coordination with 115 and the relevant authorities
• Recognizing the hazards in the surrounding area`,image:"media/images/cert_team.png"}],examples:["Follow official warnings!","Learn the warning system used in your area.","72-hour emergency kit.","The phone numbers of all family members.","2 assembly points.","2 exit routes.","CERT: community help teams.","A radio to listen for warnings."],tips:["Make a family plan.","Keep your emergency kit ready.","Practice the plan at least twice a year."],warnings:["Floods are among the most dangerous natural disasters! Just 15 cm of moving flood water can knock a person down, and 30 cm can carry a car away. Never walk or drive into flood water!"],realCase:"A family in Zakho had an emergency plan. When the flood came, they got out quickly and gathered at the assembly point. Families without a plan lose valuable time.",video:"media/videos/lesson_flood_adv.mp4",game:{instruction:"How dangerous is moving flood water? Choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"15 cm can knock a person down",emoji:"🚶",correct:!0},{text:"Shallow water is not dangerous at all",emoji:"🤷",correct:!1},{text:"30 cm can carry a car away",emoji:"🚗",correct:!0},{text:"You can drive a car through the water",emoji:"✅",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"An official flood warning tells people in the area around your home to leave. There are 2 hours left before the water reaches it. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_flood_adv.png",options:[{text:"I start the family plan, take the emergency kit, alert the family members and we go to the assembly point on high ground",correct:!0,feedback:"The official warning tells you to leave: starting the family plan, taking the kit, alerting everyone and reaching high ground saves time."},{text:"I just wait",correct:!1,feedback:"The warning tells you to leave; waiting wastes the time you have. Start the family plan and go to the assembly point."},{text:"I stay at home",correct:!1,feedback:"The warning tells you to leave; staying risks being trapped by flood water. Just 15 cm of moving water can knock a person down."},{text:"I go to the supermarket",correct:!1,feedback:"Your emergency kit should already be ready; when the warning tells you to leave, go with your family to the assembly point, not shopping."}]},quiz:[{question:"What does EWS mean?",options:[{text:"An early warning system before a disaster",correct:!0,feedback:"EWS gives early warning before a disaster, so people have time to act; follow the official warnings."},{text:"A piece of safety equipment kept in every home",correct:!1,feedback:"EWS is a warning system, not a single device: it warns people early, before a disaster."},{text:"A type of fire extinguisher used in schools",correct:!1,feedback:"EWS has nothing to do with extinguishers; it is the Early Warning System that warns before a disaster."}],explanation:"EWS (Early Warning System) warns people before a disaster, so families have time to follow their plan. Warning levels and channels differ from country to country: learn the system the authorities in your area use."},{question:"A flood warning is announced for your area. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Follow the official warnings and announcements and do what they tell you",correct:!0},{text:"Wait until you can see the water rising near your home before doing anything",correct:!1},{text:"Do whatever your neighbors decide, whatever the official announcements say",correct:!1}],explanation:"Official warnings give your family time to follow its plan. Warning levels and channels differ from country to country, so learn the system the authorities in your area use and do what it tells you."},{question:"What does the lesson teach about moving flood water?",options:[{text:"Just 15 cm of it can knock a person down, and 30 cm can carry a car away",correct:!0},{text:"It is only dangerous once it is deeper than an adult's waist, so wading is safe",correct:!1},{text:"A car is heavy enough to stay on the road in it, as long as you drive slowly",correct:!1}],explanation:"Moving flood water is much stronger than it looks: just 15 cm can knock a person down and 30 cm can carry a car away. Never walk or drive into flood water."}]},{id:7,icon:"Zap",accent:"#ca8a04",title:"Electricity: high voltage",subtitle:"Hazards and safe rescue",summary:"High-voltage electricity can kill a person and cause deep burns. Learning to recognize the hazards and the methods of safe rescue is very important.",blocks:[{heading:"The danger of high voltage",body:`• Household voltage: 220 volts; even this can kill!
• High voltage: from 11 to 400 kilovolts (kV) in power transmission lines
• It is the current (amperes) passing through the body that kills a person; high voltage drives a larger current through the body. Just 0.1 amperes (100 milliamperes) through the heart can kill
• High-voltage electricity can jump through the air (arcing) without you touching it; therefore stay at least 10 meters away
• Electrical burns are deep and may not be visible from the outside`,image:"media/images/high_voltage.png"},{heading:"Rescuing electric shock casualties",body:`1. Do not touch the casualty until the electricity has been cut off!
2. If it is high voltage: stay at least 10 meters away and call 115; only specialist teams can cut off the electricity
3. Household electricity: switch it off at the fuse, the circuit breaker or the main switch
4. If you cannot cut off the household electricity: stand on something dry and use a non-conducting object (dry wood, plastic) to move the casualty away from the source of electricity. Never do this with high voltage!
5. Call 122; if the casualty is not breathing, start CPR
6. Check for burns (where the electricity entered the body and where it left) and, after the electricity has been cut off, cool them with cool running water for 20 minutes`,image:"media/images/electric_rescue.png"},{heading:"Protecting yourself",body:`• Do not go near transformers or overhead power lines
• A power line that has fallen to the ground = danger! Stay at least 10 meters away
• If you are in a car and a line falls on it: stay inside and call 115. If the car catches fire, jump clear with both feet together, without touching the car and the ground at the same time
• If you are near a fallen line: move away with very small steps and your feet together (shuffle), without lifting your feet off the ground
• During lightning and rain: stay inside a building`,image:"media/images/electric_prevention.png"}],examples:["0.1 amperes through the heart can kill.","High voltage: stay at least 10 meters away.","First, cut off the electricity.","Use a non-conducting object (household electricity only).","If a line falls on your car, stay inside.","Small steps (shuffle step): without lifting your feet off the ground.","CPR if needed.","Call 122; for fallen lines, 115."],tips:["First, cut off the electricity.","Do not touch the casualty until the electricity has been cut off.","Use a non-conducting object.","Call 122 for the casualty and 115 for a fallen line."],warnings:["Never touch an electric shock casualty until the electricity has been cut off! The electricity will pass through your body too, and you will both become victims. Stay at least 10 meters away from high voltage!"],realCase:"In a village in Duhok, after a strong wind, a high-voltage power line fell to the ground. A trained 16-year-old knew that he must not go near it. He warned everyone to keep away and called 115. The repair teams came and removed the danger.",video:"media/videos/lesson_highvolt.mp4",game:{instruction:"When someone is getting an electric shock, which first actions are correct?",items:[{text:"Cut off the electricity",emoji:"🔌",correct:!0},{text:"Touch the casualty",emoji:"🤚",correct:!1},{text:"Call 122",emoji:"📞",correct:!0},{text:"Pull them away from the electricity with a piece of iron",emoji:"🔧",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"In the laboratory, your friend got an electric shock and collapsed. The wire is still touching his body. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_electric.png",options:[{text:"First I switch off the fuse or the circuit breaker, then, if needed, I move the wire away from him with a non-conducting object without touching his body, and I call 122; if he is not breathing, I start CPR",correct:!0,feedback:"Cutting the power first keeps you from becoming a second victim; then call 122 and start CPR if he is not breathing."},{text:"I pull him away with my hands",correct:!1,feedback:"The current would pass through you too and you would both become victims; cut off the electricity first."},{text:"I pour water on him",correct:!1,feedback:"Water conducts electricity and does not cut the current; switch off the fuse or breaker first and do not touch him."},{text:"I do nothing",correct:!1,feedback:"He needs help fast: cut off the electricity at the fuse or breaker, call 122 and start CPR if needed."}]},quiz:[{question:"How much current (amperes) through the heart can kill a person?",options:[{text:"0.1 amperes",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Only 100 milliamperes."},{text:"100 amperes",correct:!1,feedback:"Much less than this can kill!"},{text:"1000 amperes",correct:!1,feedback:"Far less is enough: just 0.1 amperes (100 milliamperes) through the heart can kill."}],explanation:"It is the current through the body that kills: just 0.1 amperes (100 milliamperes) through the heart can be deadly, and even 220-volt household electricity can kill."},{question:"A person is lying next to a fallen high-voltage power line. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Stay at least 10 meters away and call 115; only specialist teams can cut off the electricity",correct:!0},{text:"Stand on something dry and quickly push the person away from the line with a long, dry, non-conducting wooden stick",correct:!1},{text:"Pull the person away by the clothes as fast as you can, taking care not to touch the bare skin",correct:!1}],explanation:"High-voltage electricity can jump through the air, so stay at least 10 meters away and call 115. A non-conducting object is only for household electricity; never use it with high voltage."},{question:"A power line falls on the car you are in, and the car is not on fire. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Stay inside the car and call 115 for help",correct:!0},{text:"Get out at once and run away from the car fast",correct:!1},{text:"Climb out through a window and walk away",correct:!1}],explanation:"The line may be live, and inside the car you are safer: stay inside and call 115. Only if the car catches fire, jump clear with both feet together, without touching the car and the ground at the same time."}]},{id:8,icon:"Fuel",accent:"#059669",title:"Gas leaks and explosions",subtitle:"Natural gas and bottled gas (LPG)",summary:"Natural gas and bottled gas (LPG) are both flammable and explosive. This lesson explains the physical properties of gas and advanced self-protection procedures.",blocks:[{heading:"Natural gas versus LPG",body:`• Natural gas (mostly methane): lighter than air and rises upward (supplied through pipes)
• LPG (propane and butane): heavier than air and collects low down, near the floor (supplied in cylinders)
• LEL (lower explosive limit): the lowest concentration of gas in air that can catch fire or explode. UEL (upper explosive limit): above this concentration, the mixture does not catch fire
• Natural gas (methane): LEL about 5%, UEL about 15%
• LPG (propane): LEL about 2.1%, UEL about 9.5%; so a smaller concentration of LPG can explode
• Mercaptan: a distinctive smell added so that gas can be detected, because the gas itself has no smell`,image:"media/images/gas_types.png"},{heading:"Detection and procedure",body:`• Smell: a smell of rotten eggs (mercaptan)
• Sound: the hissing sound of gas from a pipe or cylinder
• Other signs: dried-out grass and trees near an underground pipe, or frost and cold at the point where LPG is leaking

Procedure:
1. Do not light any flame (matches, lighter)! Do not switch electrical switches on or off, and do not start a generator or a car nearby!
2. Do not use a phone inside
3. Open the windows and doors on your way out, if you can
4. Close the gas valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, without going into the area where the gas has collected; otherwise do not go near it
5. Get out and warn others
6. Call 115 from outside the building and notify the gas supplier`,image:"media/images/gas_procedure.png"},{heading:"Explosions and self-protection",body:`• BLEVE (Boiling Liquid Expanding Vapor Explosion): a large explosion that happens when an LPG cylinder is heated in a fire and bursts
• Fire + gas = explosion, if the gas concentration is between the LEL and the UEL

Self-protection:
• Install a gas detector at home
• Test pipes with soapy water (bubble test); never test them with a flame
• Do not use gas heaters or gas appliances in a bedroom
• Close the gas valve when you are not using it`,image:"media/images/gas_prevention.png"}],examples:["Natural gas rises / LPG collects low down.","Smell of rotten eggs = gas.","Do not strike matches or lighters!","Open the windows.","Close the gas valve only if it is safe to reach.","Call 115 from outside.","Install a gas detector.","Soapy water test (bubble test) for pipes."],tips:["Install a gas detector.","Test the pipes regularly.","Do not use gas heaters in a bedroom.","Always close the gas valve after use."],warnings:["Do not use a phone in a room full of gas! A phone can also produce a spark and cause an explosion. Call 115 from outside the building."],realCase:"In an apartment in Duhok, gas had leaked and no one had noticed. When someone switched on a light, the spark from the switch caused an explosion. If there had been a gas detector, it would have given a warning before the explosion.",video:"media/videos/lesson_gas_adv.mp4",game:{instruction:"Which way does LPG (cylinder gas) move?",items:[{text:"Downward (it is heavier than air)",emoji:"⬇️",correct:!0},{text:"Upward",emoji:"⬆️",correct:!1},{text:"It spreads evenly everywhere",emoji:"💨",correct:!1},{text:"It has no smell and cannot be detected",emoji:"👃",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"You arrive home and smell gas as soon as you open the door. The light inside is on and your phone is in your hand. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_gas_adv.png",options:[{text:"I do not go in and I do not touch the light switch; I move away from the house, warn my family and the neighbors and call 115 once I am well away from the house",correct:!0,feedback:"A switch or phone can spark in gas: stay out, warn others and call 115 once you are well away."},{text:"I touch the light switch",correct:!1,feedback:"A light switch can spark and ignite the gas, whether you switch it on or off; leave it and get out."},{text:"I use my phone inside the house",correct:!1,feedback:"A phone can also make a spark and cause an explosion; call 115 only once you are outside and away."},{text:"I stay there",correct:!1,feedback:"A single spark can make the gas explode; move away, warn your family and neighbors, then call 115 from outside."}]},quiz:[{question:"What does LEL mean?",options:[{text:"The lowest gas level in air that can explode (about 5% for natural gas)",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! For LPG (propane) it is about 2.1%."},{text:"A flammable gas used for cooking and heating, like LPG or natural gas",correct:!1,feedback:"LEL is not a gas: it is the lowest concentration of gas in air that can catch fire or explode."},{text:"A type of explosion that happens when a gas cylinder overheats in the sun",correct:!1,feedback:"No! LEL is a limit, not an explosion."}],explanation:"LEL (lower explosive limit) is the lowest concentration of gas in air that can catch fire or explode: about 5% for natural gas and about 2.1% for LPG."},{question:"You smell gas at home, and the gas valve is in the kitchen where the gas has collected. What should you do?",options:[{text:"Do not go near the valve; get out, warn others and call 115 from outside",correct:!0},{text:"Hold your breath, run into the kitchen and close the valve, then open all the windows and leave",correct:!1},{text:"Switch on the kitchen light first so that you can find the valve quickly and close it",correct:!1}],explanation:"Close the valve only if it is close and you can reach it safely, without going into the area where the gas has collected. Otherwise do not go near it: get out, warn others and call 115 from outside the building."},{question:"Why must you not use a phone in a room full of gas?",options:[{text:"It can make a spark that sets off an explosion",correct:!0},{text:"Its signal makes the gas spread faster through the room",correct:!1},{text:"The gas can damage the phone's battery and its screen",correct:!1}],explanation:"A phone, like a light switch, can make a spark, and one spark can make the gas explode. Get out first, then call 115 from outside the building."}]},{id:9,icon:"DoorOpen",accent:"#7c3aed",title:"Safe evacuation planning",subtitle:"ICS and emergency management",summary:"ICS (Incident Command System) is an international system for managing emergencies. At the high school level, students learn the ICS roles and advanced evacuation planning.",blocks:[{heading:"ICS: the command system",body:`ICS works through 5 main sections:
1. Command: the decision-maker; one person oversees all activities
2. Operations: the working teams (firefighting, rescue, first aid)
3. Planning: gathering information and drawing up the plan
4. Logistics: supplies, transport, food, facilities
5. Finance and Administration (Finance/Admin): record-keeping and expenses

At school:
• The principal or senior teacher = commander
• Teachers = operations teams
• Trained students = helpers`,image:"media/images/ics_structure.png"},{heading:"Advanced evacuation plan",body:`• Know the building: all doors, stairs and emergency exits
• Know the hazards: gas, electricity, chemicals
• Main route + alternative route
• Assembly point: main + alternative
• Counting people (headcount)
• Assigned responsibilities: who raises the alarm, who opens the doors, who counts
• People with special needs: injured people, wheelchair users, people who are blind
• Alerting: megaphone, bell, siren
• Exit doors must open from the inside without a key`,image:"media/images/evac_plan_adv.png"},{heading:"Running a proper drill",body:`• Alarm + evacuation + headcount + report
• Drill steps:
1. Sound the bell or siren
2. Stop all activities
3. Move in order to the nearest door
4. Stairs (do not use the elevator!)
5. Assembly point
6. Headcount and report
7. Do not go back inside until the "all clear" is given
• Record the evacuation time and reduce it with every drill
• Keep a record of drills`,image:"media/images/drill_procedure.png"}],examples:["ICS: Command + Operations + Planning + Logistics + Finance.","2 exit routes + 2 assembly points.","Record the evacuation time.","Counting people (headcount).","Bell or siren = alarm.","Stairs only!","Assign specific responsibilities.","Drill records."],tips:["Learn ICS.","Hold evacuation drills regularly.","The headcount is very important.","Assign specific responsibilities."],warnings:["Never use the elevator during an emergency! The elevator can stop between floors and you will be trapped inside. Always use the stairs!"],realCase:"At a school in Erbil, a teacher had learned ICS. When a fire broke out, he acted as commander and assigned roles: a team of trained staff to fight the small fire with extinguishers while it was safe, one team to evacuate the students and one team to count. Because everyone knew their role, the students got out quickly and in good order.",video:"media/videos/lesson_ics.mp4",game:{instruction:"In the ICS system, which statements are correct?",items:[{text:"Command makes the decisions",emoji:"👨‍✈️",correct:!0},{text:"Logistics is the main decision-maker",emoji:"📦",correct:!1},{text:"Operations carry out the work on site",emoji:"⚙️",correct:!0},{text:"Everyone makes decisions on their own",emoji:"🤷",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"You are the safety officer for your class. The alarm bell has rung. There are 3 students with special needs in the class. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_ics.png",options:[{text:"First I help the students with special needs, we go in order toward the stairs, I count everyone at the assembly point and report to the teacher",correct:!0,feedback:"Helping those with special needs first, moving in order by the stairs, counting at the assembly point and reporting is the plan."},{text:"I run off quickly on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"As safety officer, your class depends on you: help students with special needs, keep order and count everyone at the assembly point."},{text:"I wait in the classroom",correct:!1,feedback:"When the alarm rings, stop all activities and leave in order; waiting inside wastes time and puts the class at risk."},{text:"I use the elevator",correct:!1,feedback:"Never use the elevator in an emergency: it can stop between floors and trap you. Always use the stairs."}]},quiz:[{question:"How many main sections does ICS have?",options:[{text:"5",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics, Finance/Admin."},{text:"3",correct:!1,feedback:"ICS has 5 main sections, not 3: Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics and Finance/Admin."},{text:"10",correct:!1,feedback:"ICS has only 5 main sections: Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics and Finance/Admin."}],explanation:"ICS has 5 main sections: Command, Operations, Planning, Logistics and Finance/Admin. At school, the principal is the commander, teachers are operations teams and trained students are helpers."},{question:"In a drill, what is done after reaching the assembly point?",options:[{text:"A headcount and a report",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! So that we know no one is left inside the building."},{text:"Going straight back to the classroom",correct:!1,feedback:'No! Do not go back until the "all clear" is given.'},{text:"Every student goes home",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Everyone must be counted."}]},{question:"In an evacuation plan, how must exit doors open?",options:[{text:"From the inside, without a key",correct:!0},{text:"Only with a key kept by the principal",correct:!1},{text:"Only from the outside, by the safety team",correct:!1}],explanation:"In an emergency there is no time to look for a key: exit doors must open from the inside without a key so that everyone can get out quickly."}]},{id:10,icon:"Droplets",accent:"#dc2626",title:"Bleeding and fractures",subtitle:"Advanced first aid",summary:"Severe bleeding (hemorrhage) and broken bones (fractures) are among the most dangerous injuries. The right action in the first minutes can make the difference between life and death. This lesson follows the Stop the Bleed program.",blocks:[{heading:"Severe bleeding and how to stop it",body:`Types of bleeding:
• Arterial: bright red, spurting and pulsing with the heartbeat. The most dangerous!
• Venous: dark red, flows steadily
• Capillary (from very fine blood vessels): slow, drop by drop

Stopping the bleeding (Stop the Bleed):
1. Protect yourself (put on gloves) and call 122, or ask someone else to call
2. Direct pressure: place a clean cloth on the wound and press hard with both hands without stopping
3. If the cloth becomes soaked with blood, do not remove it; place another cloth on top and keep up the pressure
4. Deep wound (in the thigh, armpit or neck): fill the wound with clean gauze or cloth (Wound Packing), then press hard. This is not for chest or abdominal wounds
5. Life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg: apply a tourniquet early, 5–7 cm above the wound`,image:"media/images/bleeding_control.png"},{heading:"Fractures",body:`Types of fracture:
• Closed: the bone is broken but the skin is intact
• Open: the bone breaks through the skin and comes out; more dangerous!

Signs:
• Pain, swelling, bruising
• Abnormal shape of the limb, inability to move it
• A cracking sound at the moment of injury

Help:
1. Do not move the injured limb!
2. Splint: immobilize the limb in the position it is in
3. Immobilize the joints above and below the fracture
4. Open fracture: press around the wound to stop the bleeding and cover the wound with a clean cloth
5. Do not try to push the protruding bone back into place!
6. Call 122`,image:"media/images/fracture_care.png"},{heading:"The tourniquet",body:`A tourniquet is used for life-threatening limb bleeding (bleeding from an arm or leg). If blood is spurting forcefully or pressure is not enough, use it early; do not leave it until last.

Position: 5–7 cm above the wound (between the wound and the body), not over a joint. If the location of the wound is unclear, apply it as high as possible on the limb.
How:
1. Wrap the tourniquet around the limb
2. Pull the free end tight and fasten it
3. Turn the rod (Windlass) until the bleeding stops completely
4. Lock the rod in place
5. Write down the time the tourniquet was applied!

A tourniquet causes pain; this is normal. Never loosen or remove it; only medical staff remove it.`,image:"media/images/tourniquet.png"}],examples:["Arterial = bright red + spurting.","Venous = dark red + steady flow.","Firm, direct pressure = the first step.","Cloth soaked with blood? Do not remove it; put another one on top.","Deep wound: wound packing + pressure.","Tourniquet: 5–7 cm above the wound, early.","Closed versus open fracture.","Write down the time the tourniquet was applied and never loosen it."],tips:["Direct pressure is the first action.","Do not let go of the pressure!","Life-threatening bleeding from an arm or leg: tourniquet early.","Write down the time it was applied."],warnings:["Once a tourniquet has been applied, never loosen or remove it! Only medical staff remove it. Write the time of application on the tourniquet or on the casualty's forehead."],realCase:"In Sulaymaniyah, a Grade 11 student who had learned first aid saw blood spurting from his friend's wound (arterial bleeding). He immediately applied firm, direct pressure to the wound and did not let go until the ambulance arrived, while another friend called 122. In arterial bleeding, every minute counts.",video:"media/videos/lesson_bleeding.mp4",game:{instruction:"About bleeding, which statements are correct?",items:[{text:"Arterial blood: bright red and spurting",emoji:"❤️",correct:!0},{text:"Arterial blood: dark red and slow",emoji:"🔵",correct:!1},{text:"Firm, direct pressure is the first step",emoji:"✋",correct:!0},{text:"Pour water on the wound to stop the bleeding",emoji:"💧",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"In the laboratory, a student's hand has been cut by glass and is bleeding heavily. The blood is spurting and bright red. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_bleeding.png",options:[{text:"I put on gloves, apply firm, direct pressure with a clean cloth and do not let go, and I call out for help so that the teacher calls 122 and brings the first aid kit. If the bleeding does not stop, I apply a tourniquet 5–7 cm above the wound and write down the time",correct:!0,feedback:"Firm, direct pressure comes first; if the spurting bleeding does not stop, apply a tourniquet early, 5–7 cm above the wound."},{text:"I pour water on the wound",correct:!1,feedback:"Water does not stop bleeding; firm, direct pressure with a clean cloth is the first step."},{text:"I let the bleeding stop by itself",correct:!1,feedback:"Spurting, bright red blood is arterial bleeding, the most dangerous kind, and every minute counts: press hard now."},{text:"I run away",correct:!1,feedback:"Your classmate needs help now: put on gloves, press hard on the wound and call out so that someone calls 122."}]},quiz:[{question:"Where is a tourniquet applied?",options:[{text:"5–7 cm above the wound, between the wound and the body",correct:!0,feedback:"Correct! And write down the time it was applied."},{text:"Directly on the wound itself, tied as tightly as you can",correct:!1,feedback:"The tourniquet goes 5–7 cm above the wound, between the wound and the body; direct pressure is what goes on the wound."},{text:"On the nearest joint, such as the knee or the elbow",correct:!1,feedback:"No! Not on a joint; apply it above the joint."}],explanation:"Apply a tourniquet 5–7 cm above the wound, between the wound and the body, never over a joint; if the wound's location is unclear, apply it as high as possible. Write down the time."},{question:"What does an open fracture mean?",options:[{text:"The bone breaks through the skin and comes out",correct:!0,feedback:"The bone breaks through the skin, which makes an open fracture more dangerous than a closed one."},{text:"The bone is broken but the skin is intact",correct:!1,feedback:"That is a closed fracture!"},{text:"There is pain and swelling, but the bone is not broken",correct:!1,feedback:"Pain is one sign of a fracture; an open fracture means the bone has broken through the skin."}],explanation:"In an open fracture the bone breaks through the skin and comes out, which is more dangerous than a closed fracture, where the skin is intact. Never push the bone back."},{question:"The cloth you are pressing on a wound becomes soaked with blood. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Leave it, put another cloth on top and keep pressing",correct:!0},{text:"Take it off and press again firmly with a fresh, clean, dry cloth",correct:!1},{text:"Lift it to check whether the bleeding has stopped yet",correct:!1}],explanation:"Do not remove a soaked cloth: lifting it stops the pressure on the wound. Put another cloth on top and keep up firm, direct pressure without stopping."}]},{id:11,icon:"ShieldAlert",accent:"#db2777",title:"International safety signs",subtitle:"GHS, NFPA and ISO",summary:"International safety signs (GHS, NFPA 704 and ISO 7010) are a worldwide language for warnings. In this lesson we learn about these systems and standards.",blocks:[{heading:"GHS: the global system",body:`GHS (Globally Harmonized System) has 9 main pictograms:
1. Explosive: exploding bomb
2. Flammable: flame
3. Oxidiser (Oxidizer): flame over circle
4. Gas under pressure (Compressed Gas): gas cylinder
5. Corrosive: drops on a hand and on metal
6. Deadly poison (Acute Toxicity): skull and crossbones
7. Irritant and minor harm (Irritant/Harmful): exclamation mark (!)
8. Serious health hazard (Health Hazard): human body with a star
9. Hazard to the environment (Environmental): fish and tree

Each pictogram is black, on a white background, inside a diamond with a red border. GHS has no pictogram for radioactive materials; that symbol is found in ISO 7010 and in the transport regulations.`,image:"media/images/ghs_all.png"},{heading:"The NFPA 704 diamond",body:`The NFPA 704 diamond is made up of 4 sections:
• Red (top) = flammability: 0–4 (0 = does not burn, 4 = extremely flammable)
• Blue (left) = health: 0–4 (0 = no hazard, 4 = deadly)
• Yellow (right) = instability: 0–4 (0 = stable, 4 = may explode)
• White (bottom) = special hazards: OX = oxidizer, W with a line through it = do not use water, SA = simple asphyxiant gas

Example: gasoline = red 3, blue 1, yellow 0`,image:"media/images/nfpa_diamond.png"},{heading:"ISO 7010 signs",body:`ISO 7010 is the international standard for safety signs:
• Prohibition: a red circle with a red diagonal line. Examples: "No entry", "No smoking"
• Warning: a yellow triangle with a black border. Examples: general danger, electricity, radioactive material
• Mandatory: a blue circle. Examples: "Wear eye protection", "Wear gloves"
• Safe condition: a green square. Examples: emergency exit, first aid, emergency telephone
• Fire equipment: a red square. Examples: fire extinguisher, fire hose`,image:"media/images/iso_signs.png"}],examples:["GHS: skull = toxic.","NFPA red = flammability.","NFPA blue = health.","NFPA yellow = instability.","NFPA white = special hazards (OX, W, SA).","ISO: red circle = prohibited.","ISO: yellow triangle = warning.","ISO: green square = safety."],tips:["Learn the 9 GHS pictograms.","Know the 4 colors of NFPA 704.","ISO: red = prohibition, yellow = warning, blue = mandatory, green = safety."],warnings:["Take signs seriously! They are there to protect your life. If you see a sign you do not recognize, do not go near it and ask specialists."],realCase:"At a factory in Zakho, a new worker had not read the NFPA diamond on a chemical tank. He tried to put out a fire with water, but the white section was marked with a W with a line through it (do not use water!). The fire grew larger. If he had recognized the sign, this incident would not have happened.",video:"media/videos/lesson_symbols.mp4",game:{instruction:"Which statements are correct? Choose the correct ones:",items:[{text:"NFPA: blue = health",emoji:"🔵",correct:!0},{text:"NFPA: red = instability",emoji:"🔴",correct:!1},{text:"ISO: green = safety",emoji:"🟢",correct:!0},{text:"NFPA: yellow = flammability",emoji:"🟡",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"In the laboratory there is a tank with an NFPA diamond: red 4, blue 3, yellow 2, white OX. What does it mean, and how will you handle it?",image:"media/images/activity_symbols.png",options:[{text:"It is extremely flammable (4), very dangerous to health (3), moderately unstable (2) and an oxidizer (OX). I wear the appropriate PPE and handle it carefully; I keep it away from fire and flammable materials",correct:!0,feedback:"Red 4: extremely flammable; blue 3: very dangerous to health; yellow 2: moderately unstable; OX: oxidizer. So keep it away from fire."},{text:"It is safe",correct:!1,feedback:"These are high numbers on a 0–4 scale: red 4 means extremely flammable and blue 3 very dangerous to health."},{text:"I do not know",correct:!1,feedback:"Each color and number has a meaning you can learn; if you do not recognize a sign, keep away and ask specialists."},{text:"I pour water on it",correct:!1,feedback:"Do not guess: read the whole diamond first (a W with a line through it means no water) and ask specialists."}]},quiz:[{question:"How many main pictograms does GHS have?",options:[{text:"9",correct:!0,feedback:"GHS has 9 pictograms, from the exploding bomb and the flame to the fish and tree for environmental hazards."},{text:"4",correct:!1,feedback:"Those are the 4 colors of NFPA!"},{text:"6",correct:!1,feedback:"GHS has 9 main pictograms, not 6; learn all nine, from the exploding bomb to the fish and tree."}],explanation:"GHS has 9 main pictograms, each black on a white background inside a diamond with a red border, such as the flame, the skull and crossbones and the exclamation mark."},{question:"In ISO, what does a yellow triangle mean?",options:[{text:"Warning and danger",correct:!0,feedback:"A yellow triangle with a black border warns of danger, such as electricity or radioactive material."},{text:"Prohibition of an action",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the red circle!"},{text:"Safe condition or exit",correct:!1,feedback:"That is the green square!"}],explanation:"In ISO 7010, a yellow triangle with a black border means warning, such as general danger or electricity. A red circle means prohibition, a blue circle mandatory and a green square safe condition."},{question:"In the white section of the NFPA 704 diamond, what does a W with a line through it mean?",options:[{text:"Do not use water on this material",correct:!0},{text:"The material dissolves easily in water",correct:!1},{text:"Wear waterproof gloves when handling it",correct:!1}],explanation:"A W with a line through it means: do not use water. Read the whole diamond before you act, and if you see a sign you do not recognize, do not go near it and ask specialists."}]},{id:12,icon:"Users",accent:"#4f46e5",title:"Leadership in emergencies",subtitle:"Decision-making and team leadership",summary:"Leadership in emergencies is a special task: making decisions quickly and under pressure, leading the team and coordinating with institutions. This lesson prepares students to become safety leaders.",blocks:[{heading:"Decision-making in emergencies",body:`The OODA Loop model:
• Observe: look at the situation around you and assess it
• Orient: analyze the information and identify the hazards
• Decide: choose a quick and safe plan
• Act: carry out the plan

Principles:
• Decide quickly; an imperfect decision is better than no decision
• Gather information, but do not wait for all of it
• Keep the team informed
• Keep watching, and change the plan if needed`,image:"media/images/ooda_loop.png"},{heading:"Team leadership",body:`• Assign roles: everyone should know what they will do
• Clear communication: give short, clear instructions
• Listen: the team gives you information
• Calm: if you are afraid, the team will be afraid too
• Support: help team members and back them up

At school:
• You = the class safety officer
• Roles: raising the alarm, evacuation, headcount, first aid
• Regular practice`,image:"media/images/team_leadership.png"},{heading:"Coordination and communication",body:`In an emergency, communication is the most important thing.

Emergency numbers in the Kurdistan Region:
• 115 = Civil Defense (fire, rescue, disasters)
• 122 = Ambulance (medical emergencies)
• 104 = Police

Alerting:
• Megaphone / siren
• Phones and messaging groups
• Radio

Report (when you call 115):
• Where? Say the exact location first: area, street, a nearby landmark and the floor
• What happened?
• How many people are hurt, trapped or affected?
• Any danger: fire spreading, gas, electricity, chemicals?
• What help is needed?
• Your name and your phone number
• Do not hang up until they tell you to`,image:"media/images/communication.png"}],examples:["OODA: Observe-Orient-Decide-Act.","Assign roles.","Short, clear instructions.","Keep your voice calm.","115 fire and rescue, 122 ambulance, 104 police.","Report: what + where + how many + what is needed.","Keep watching and change the plan if needed.","Communication and alerting = life."],tips:["Learn the OODA Loop.","Assign roles.","Stay calm.","Clear communication."],warnings:["Leadership in an emergency does not mean putting yourself in danger! It means leading the team wisely and calmly and making the right decisions."],realCase:"At a school in Sulaymaniyah, a Grade 12 student who had learned leadership skills calmly led her class when an earthquake struck: during the shaking everyone took cover under the desks, and after the shaking stopped she assigned roles and helped the students with special needs, and everyone reached the assembly point in good order. The teacher said that her calmness helped a great deal that day.",video:"media/videos/lesson_leadership.mp4",game:{instruction:"In the OODA Loop, what is the first step?",items:[{text:"Observe",emoji:"👀",correct:!0},{text:"Act",emoji:"🏃",correct:!1},{text:"Decide",emoji:"🧠",correct:!1},{text:"Orient",emoji:"🗺️",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"You are in a lesson and you hear a loud explosion from the chemistry laboratory. The teacher is not there. You are the class safety officer and there are 18 students in the class. What will you do?",image:"media/images/activity_leadership.png",options:[{text:"Using OODA: I assess the situation, assign tasks (alerting + evacuation + helping), we leave in order by the stairs toward the assembly point, I count everyone and call 115 and 122",correct:!0,feedback:"OODA in action: assess, assign roles, leave in order by the stairs, count everyone and call 115 and 122."},{text:"I run off quickly on my own",correct:!1,feedback:"As safety officer, 18 classmates depend on you; leading calmly and assigning roles gets everyone out, not just you."},{text:"I go to the chemistry laboratory",correct:!1,feedback:"Leadership does not mean putting yourself in danger; never go toward an explosion. Lead the class out and call 115 and 122."},{text:"I just wait",correct:!1,feedback:"Waiting wastes vital time; decide quickly, because an imperfect decision is better than no decision. Lead the class out."}]},quiz:[{question:"How many steps make up the OODA Loop?",options:[{text:"4: Observe, Orient, Decide, Act",correct:!0,feedback:"Observe, Orient, Decide, Act: four steps, repeated as you keep watching and change the plan if needed."},{text:"3: Observe, Decide, Act",correct:!1,feedback:"One step is missing: the OODA Loop has 4 steps, Observe, Orient, Decide and Act."},{text:"6: Plan, Observe, Orient, Decide, Act, Review",correct:!1,feedback:"The OODA Loop has only 4 steps: Observe, Orient, Decide and Act."}],explanation:"The OODA Loop has 4 steps: Observe the situation, Orient (analyze and identify hazards), Decide on a quick, safe plan and Act. Then keep watching and change the plan if needed."},{question:"In an emergency, what is the most important thing?",options:[{text:"Clear and fast communication",correct:!0,feedback:"Clear, fast communication tells everyone what to do and gets the right help, starting with the exact location for 115."},{text:"Getting away from the scene on your own",correct:!1,feedback:"A leader does not run off alone; leadership means guiding the team calmly and keeping everyone informed."},{text:"Waiting until you have all the information",correct:!1,feedback:"Decide quickly: an imperfect decision is better than no decision, and you should not wait for all the information."}],explanation:"In an emergency, communication is the most important thing: short, clear instructions tell the team what to do, and a clear report to 115 brings the right help."},{question:"When you call 115, what do you say first?",options:[{text:"The exact location: area, street, a nearby landmark and the floor",correct:!0},{text:"Your full name, your phone number and the name of your school",correct:!1},{text:"Everything that happened, from the very beginning, in as much detail as you can",correct:!1}],explanation:"Say the exact location first, so that help reaches the right place even if the call is cut off; then say what happened, how many people are hurt, any dangers, the help needed and your name and number. Do not hang up until they tell you to."}]},{id:13,icon:"Bomb",accent:"#b45309",title:"Mines and unexploded ordnance for teenagers",subtitle:"Curiosity, friends and social media",summary:'Mines and unexploded ordnance can stay dangerous for decades. Teenagers are curious, go out with friends and share a lot online. This lesson shows how to stay safe, how to say "no" to risky ideas and how to protect younger children.',blocks:[{heading:"Why they are still dangerous",body:"Explosive ordnance comes in many sizes, shapes and colors. It can be buried, hidden in grass, rubble or old buildings, or lying in the open. Old, rusty or broken items can still explode, and an item that has been moved once without exploding can explode the next time."},{heading:"The safety rules",points:["Do not touch, move, kick or pick it up.","Do not throw anything at it and never try to burn it.","Do not go closer to look, film or take a selfie.","Warn others and keep them away, especially younger children.","Move well away, then call 115 or tell an adult; describe the place and nearby landmarks.","Respect signs, fences and red stones; no sign does not mean safe."]},{heading:"Curiosity, friends and social media",body:'Friends may dare you to touch an object, keep one as a souvenir, pose next to a destroyed military vehicle or sell metal for pocket money. Saying "no" is not weak: it protects you and them.',points:[`Say clearly: "No. It can explode. Let's move away and tell someone."`,"If your friends do not listen, leave and tell an adult at once.","Never collect or sell military scrap: it endangers you, the buyer and the whole neighborhood.","Do not share videos or dares of people handling explosive objects; show them to an adult instead.","Do not believe or spread rumors that a place is safe; follow official announcements."]},{heading:"If you are in a dangerous area",body:`If you see a mine, a warning sign or a strange object near your feet, or there has been an explosion:
1. Stop at once and stand still; do not move your feet.
2. Call out for help and tell others not to come to you.
3. Wait for trained help, even if it takes a long time.

Never try to walk back in your footprints, test the ground or place marks. If someone is hurt, do not run to them: call for help and wait.`},{heading:"Helping younger children",points:['Teach them the simple rule: "If you did not drop it, do not pick it up."',"Show them the safe paths to school and to play, and the places to avoid.","Tell them to come to you or another adult at once if they see something strange.","Set a good example: younger children copy what older ones do."]}],examples:['A friend dares you to kick a rusty shell = say "no", move away together and tell an adult.',"Someone wants a selfie on a destroyed tank = keep away; explosive items may be in or around it.","A video of someone holding a grenade is shared in a group = do not share it; tell an adult.","A field with no signs near an old front line = not necessarily safe; use well-used paths.","A younger brother finds a colorful object = keep him away from it and tell an adult.","You see a strange object while out with friends = move well away, then call 115."],tips:["Save 115 in your phone.","When you go out with friends, stay on main roads and well-used paths.","Find out which places near your home and school are dangerous.","Talk with your family about what to do if someone finds a strange object."],warnings:["Never touch, move, collect, film up close or sell explosive ordnance or military scrap!","In a dangerous area: stop, stand still, call for help and wait; never walk back in your footprints!"],realCase:'A group of friends was walking along a road near an old army position. One of them saw a rusty object at the edge of the road and wanted to pick it up for a video. Another friend said, "No, it can explode," and they all went on along the road without touching it. At home, he told his father, who called 115 and described the place and the nearest landmark.',media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_uxo_teens_say_no.webp",title:"Say no and move away",description:"Teenagers stop a friend from going closer to a rusty object and move away along the road."}],game:{instruction:"Which of these are safe choices?",items:[{text:'Say "no" to the dare',emoji:"🙅",correct:!0},{text:"Go closer to take a photo",emoji:"📸",correct:!1},{text:"Call 115 once you are far away",emoji:"📞",correct:!0},{text:"Sell it as scrap",emoji:"💰",correct:!1},{text:"Stay on well-used roads and paths",emoji:"🛣️",correct:!0},{text:"Throw stones at it",emoji:"🪨",correct:!1}]},activity:{prompt:"Your friends find an old shell next to a ruined building. One of them wants to post a video of himself holding it, and another says a scrap dealer would pay for it. What do you do?",options:[{text:'I say "no", we all move away without touching it, and from a safe place I call 115 or tell an adult',correct:!0,feedback:'Saying "no" and moving away protects everyone, and reporting from a safe place lets trained teams deal with the shell.'},{text:"I film it from close up as a warning to others, without touching it",correct:!1,feedback:"Going closer to film is dangerous even without touching. Warn others from far away and report it."},{text:"I let them carry it to the scrap dealer, as long as they walk slowly",correct:!1,feedback:"Carrying it can make it explode and puts the dealer and the neighbors in danger too. Leave it and report it."},{text:"I cover it with stones so that younger children do not find it",correct:!1,feedback:"Covering or marking it means staying next to the danger. Move away, keep others away and report it."}]},quiz:[{question:"A friend says an old, rusty shell is harmless. What is true?",options:[{text:"Old and rusty ordnance can still explode, even after decades",correct:!0},{text:"Rust shows that the explosive inside has already become completely harmless",correct:!1},{text:"It is harmless if someone has already moved it from its place without an explosion",correct:!1},{text:"It is harmless if it is smaller than the palm of your hand",correct:!1}],explanation:"Mines and unexploded ordnance stay dangerous for decades and can look like harmless scrap. An item that was moved once can explode the next time, and small items are dangerous too."},{question:"What is the safest way to share information about an object you saw?",options:[{text:"From far away, tell 115 or an adult the place and landmarks; do not go back for photos",correct:!0},{text:"Go back with a friend to take clear photos of it and post them in the class group",correct:!1},{text:"Post the exact place online so that other people can go there and see it for themselves",correct:!1},{text:"Wait a few days to see whether it is still there, then tell someone",correct:!1}],explanation:"Report quickly from a safe place: the place and nearby landmarks are what the team needs. Going back for photos or sending others to look puts people in danger."},{question:"You realize you may be inside a mined area. What do you do?",options:[{text:"Stop, stand still, call for help and wait",correct:!0},{text:"Walk back carefully in your own footprints to the road",correct:!1},{text:"Test the ground in front of you with a stick to find a safe path",correct:!1},{text:"Run to the nearest road as fast as you can",correct:!1}],explanation:"Standing still keeps you from stepping on another mine. Footprints are hard to see, testing the ground is a job for specialists and running is extremely dangerous."},{question:"Why should you never collect or sell military scrap?",options:[{text:"It can explode at any time and endanger you, the buyer and people nearby",correct:!0},{text:"Dealers pay much less for metal that has been lying outside in the rain for a long time",correct:!1},{text:"The metal is very heavy and can hurt your back when you carry it",correct:!1},{text:"It must be cleaned before it is sold, and cleaning takes too long",correct:!1}],explanation:"Unexploded ordnance can look like harmless scrap but remains extremely dangerous. Collecting, carrying or selling it puts you, the buyer and the whole neighborhood at risk."}]},{id:14,icon:"LifeBuoy",accent:"#0369a1",title:"Water safety for teenagers",subtitle:"Open water, friends and safe rescue",summary:"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), children and young people are among those most affected by drowning. Teenagers often swim with friends in rivers, dams and canals, where nobody may be watching and help is far away. In this lesson, you learn the hidden dangers of open water, how to refuse a dare, how to help a friend without going in and what to do if you fall in.",blocks:[{heading:"Hidden dangers of open water",body:`Rivers, dams and canals are not swimming pools:
• Currents: the water can be fast and strong, even when the surface looks calm.
• Cold water: sudden cold water makes you gasp and breathe fast, and you can breathe in water. Even strong swimmers get into difficulty.
• Banks: steep and slippery banks can make it very hard to get out.
• Depth and hidden objects: it is hard to judge how deep the water is, and rocks, metal or branches can be hidden under the surface.`},{heading:"Dares and pressure from friends",body:`A dare to jump from a height, to swim across a river or to stay in the water longer can end in drowning or a serious injury. The World Health Organization links higher drowning rates among males to riskier behavior, such as swimming alone.
• You can say no. A real friend does not push you into danger.
• Agree on the rules before you go: nobody swims alone, nobody swims after dark and nobody jumps into water of unknown depth.
• If a friend is taking a risk, speak up and tell an adult.`},{heading:"Rules for swimming and boats",body:`• Never swim alone. Swim where a lifeguard or an adult who can help is watching.
• Do not swim after dark: nobody can see you, and you cannot see the dangers.
• Never jump or dive into water when you do not know how deep it is. Hitting a rock or the bottom can injure the spinal cord and leave you paralyzed, sometimes for life.
• In a boat, always wear a life jacket, whether or not you can swim.
• Obey no-swimming signs and the instructions of Civil Defense and official announcements.`},{heading:"Helping a friend safely",body:`Do not go into the water unless you are trained and equipped for water rescue; otherwise you can drown too. Reach or throw, do not go:
1. Shout for help and call 115 and 122. Say exactly where you are.
2. Reach: from firm ground on the bank, hold out a long stick, a branch or a rope.
3. Throw: throw something that floats, such as a life ring, a ball or an empty plastic container with its lid on.
4. When the person is out of the water and is not breathing normally, start CPR: with breaths if you are trained, chest compressions only if you are not, and follow the dispatcher's instructions.
5. Everyone pulled from the water must be checked by a doctor, even if they seem well.`},{heading:"If you fall in: float to live",body:`Cold water shock makes you gasp and want to thrash around. Fight that instinct:
• Lean back, with your ears in the water and your face up.
• Spread your arms and legs, and move your hands gently to stay afloat.
• Stay calm and float until your breathing is under control.
• Then shout and signal for help, hold on to something that floats or swim to safety if you can.`}],examples:["Calm surface, strong current underneath.","Cold water: gasping and fast breathing, so float first.","Unknown depth: no jumping and no diving.","Boat: life jacket on, whether or not you can swim.","Friend in trouble: call, reach or throw, do not go.","A dare is never a reason to risk your life."],tips:["Swim only where a lifeguard or an adult who can help is watching.","Learn to swim and learn safe rescue skills with a trained instructor.","Learn CPR in a first aid course.","Save 115 and 122 in your phone and know how to describe where you are."],warnings:["Never go into the water to rescue someone unless you are trained: reach or throw, do not go.","Never jump or dive into water of unknown depth: a spinal cord injury can leave you paralyzed."],realCase:"On a hot afternoon, a group of teenagers went to a river. One of them dared the others to jump into the water from a high rock. A girl in the group said no and reminded them that nobody knew how deep the water was. One boy jumped anyway, came up gasping in the cold current and could not reach the bank. The girl did not go in. She shouted for help and called 115 and 122, while a friend held out a long branch from firm ground and another threw him an empty plastic container with its lid on. He held on to it until adults pulled him out, and he was checked at the hospital.",media:[{kind:"image",src:"media/images/w7_teen_reach_or_throw.webp",title:"Reach or throw, do not go",description:"Teenagers help a friend from the bank: one calls for help, one holds out a long branch and one throws something that floats."}],game:{instruction:"Safe or dangerous? Choose the safe choices:",items:[{text:"Swimming where a lifeguard is watching",emoji:"👀",correct:!0},{text:"Accepting a dare to swim across a river",emoji:"🌊",correct:!1},{text:"Wearing a life jacket in a boat",emoji:"🚣",correct:!0},{text:"Diving where you cannot see the bottom",emoji:"🤿",correct:!1},{text:"Swimming with friends after dark",emoji:"🌙",correct:!1},{text:"Floating on your back after falling into cold water",emoji:"🛟",correct:!0}]},activity:{prompt:"You are at a dam with friends. One friend slips from the bank into deep, cold water and is struggling to stay up. None of you is trained in water rescue. What do you do?",options:[{text:"I stay on firm ground, shout for help, call 115 and 122, and reach with a long branch or throw something that floats",correct:!0,feedback:"Calling brings trained rescuers and an ambulance, and reaching or throwing helps your friend without putting a second person in the water."},{text:"I jump in at once, because I am a strong swimmer",correct:!1,feedback:"Cold water shock and currents can overwhelm strong swimmers too, and you could drown with your friend. Call, then reach or throw; do not go."},{text:"We hold hands in a chain and walk into the water to reach our friend",correct:!1,feedback:"On a slippery bank and in deep, cold water, the whole chain can be pulled in. Stay on firm ground and reach with an object or throw something that floats."},{text:"I run to look for an adult and call nobody until I find one",correct:!1,feedback:"Every second counts. Shout for help and call 115 and 122 right away, then reach or throw from the bank."}]},quiz:[{question:"Why is it dangerous to jump into a river or dam when you do not know how deep it is?",options:[{text:"Hidden rocks or shallow places can cause a spinal cord injury",correct:!0},{text:"It is only dangerous for people who cannot swim well",correct:!1},{text:"It is only dangerous in winter, when the water in rivers is cold",correct:!1},{text:"It is only dangerous in swimming pools without a lifeguard",correct:!1}],explanation:"You cannot see what is under the water, and it is hard to judge depth. Jumping or diving into water that is too shallow is a major cause of spinal cord injury, which can leave a person paralyzed. Never jump or dive into water of unknown depth."},{question:"You fall into cold, deep water. What should you do first?",options:[{text:"Lean back and float, and control your breathing before you swim",correct:!0},{text:"Swim as hard as you can to the bank before your body gets cold",correct:!1},{text:"Hold your breath and dive down to find the bottom with your feet",correct:!1}],explanation:"Cold water shock makes you gasp and breathe fast, even if you are a strong swimmer. Float on your back until your breathing is under control; then shout for help or swim to safety."},{question:"A friend is struggling in a canal and you are not trained in water rescue. What is the safest action?",options:[{text:"Call 115 and 122, then reach with a stick or throw something that floats",correct:!0},{text:"Jump in and pull your friend out, because there is no time to call",correct:!1},{text:"Walk into the water up to your waist and hold out your hand to your friend",correct:!1},{text:"Call only your parents and wait for them to decide what to do",correct:!1}],explanation:"Reach or throw, do not go: an untrained rescuer who goes into the water can drown too. Calling 115 and 122 brings trained rescuers and an ambulance."},{question:"When should you wear a life jacket in a boat?",options:[{text:"Every time, whether or not you can swim",correct:!0},{text:"Only when the weather is stormy or windy",correct:!1},{text:"Only if you cannot swim, or if you swim badly",correct:!1},{text:"Only when the boat goes far from the bank",correct:!1}],explanation:"Life jackets reduce the risk of drowning while boating for people of all ages and swimming abilities. Wear one every time you are in a boat."}]}],t={lessons:e};export{t as default,e as lessons};
